text:
jen: Quick thread: breadcrumb canonicalization + back-button behavior for deep links into compact nav. I sketched interactions because there's a subtle UX/instrumentation tradeoff.

jen: Problem: when we resolve a requested deep link to a different canonical route (eg legacy alias -> new item route), we replaceState to canonicalize the URL. that makes sense for sharing, but breaks expectation when user hits back (they expect original link). need clear policy.

omar: Repro: open a shared link /org/x/ws/alpha/items/old-42 -> resolver redirects to /org/x/ws/alpha/collections/42/overview and we replaceState. hitting back goes to previous app page (not the original alias) because we used replaceState and didn't push. confusing when user expects to return to email.

kiara: Proposal: use replaceState for silent canonicalization in most cases, but if there is meaningful semantic change (alias -> different resource) then pushState the original first and then replaceState to canonical. That preserves back button to the external origin while keeping canonical URL in address bar.

ev an: Slight variant: always replaceState, but add an ephemeral history entry when resolvedRoute !== requestedRoute: history.pushState({orig: requested}, "", requested); history.replaceState({}, "", resolved); This keeps a back entry to requestedRoute but prevents duplicating in server logs. Need to be careful with SPA routing and popstate handlers.

jen: I sketched the flow (pseudo):
```
// on deep-link load
const requested = normalize(window.location.pathname + window.location.search + window.location.hash);
const resolved = resolveNav(requested);
if (resolved !== requested) {
  // keep a back-step to original but canonicalize
  history.pushState({via: 'orig'}, '', requested);
  history.replaceState({}, '', resolved);
  showBannerOnce('We opened the closest match. Open original', {action: () => navigate(requested)});
} else {
  history.replaceState({}, '', resolved); // no-op usually
}
```

kiara: Accessibility: the banner needs role="status" and aria-live="polite" plus link with aria-describedby pointing to the banner so screen readers can discover it. When we programmatically move focus to the resolved item, ensure we don't mask the banner announcement. maybe focus the resolved item and use a short live-region message: "Opened closest match, press link in the banner to open original."

omar: Breadcrumbs: they should reflect resolvedResource hierarchy, not the requested alias. But the banner copy should show the original breadcrumb path and offer "Open original" so users understand the difference. Eg breadcrumb: Collections > Revenue dashboard (resolved) and banner: "You opened Revenue dashboard from Legacy Reports > Feb 2025". keep banner small.

jen: Copy nits: prefer "Showing closest match" over "You were routed to the closest match" for brevity. CTA: "Open original". Banner should be dismissible and dismissed state stored in sessionStorage keyed by original path so we don't repeatedly nag on refresh.

ev an: Back-button QA: test scenario with external referrer (email), internal navigation chain, and when user used keyboard shortcut to focus nav. We must ensure popstate handlers don't double-resolve and that analytics fire only once. add unit tests for push/replace/pop scenarios.

kiara: Edge cases: 1) hash anchors to sections (/#metrics) — normalize by converting hash -> ?anchor=metrics so server-side logging sees it. 2) trailing slash diffs — normalize. 3) ambiguous slugs where group and child share slug — resolver must prefer exact param match; add disambiguation rules.

omar: Where to house alias map? keep in nav manifest with tests; backend can expose short-lived redirects for major migrations. devex owns manifest. runtime should validate collisions and warn during build.

jen: Small accessibility snippet I'll paste in code later: set aria-current="page" on resolved item link, set aria-expanded on group toggles, use a live region id for banner with aria-live and aria-atomic. sample snippet will include showBannerOnce logic.

ev an: Instrumentation: track events when canonicalization happens (resolved_from, resolved_to, via_banner_shown, banner_action). also track how often users click "Open original" so we can decide whether to create permanent redirects.

nav-bot: Preview: https://console-preview.redwood/nav/breadcrumb-canonical (ephemeral)

jen: Action items: @omar prototype history push/replace variant and test popstate flows; @kiara write banner a11y snippet + copy options; @evan add analytics spec and QA test matrix; jen will update figma with banner + breadcrumb examples. we'll review Tue design crit. thanks :thumbsup:
